                <pb facs="0411" n="311" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg311"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>No official record of the substance of this conversation has been found. The fact
                    that Hurley had an appointment with
                        Chiang on November 23 is indicated
                    in a letter from Hurley’s aide (Major
                        John Henry) to Hopkins, dated November 23, 1943 (Hopkins Papers).</p>
                <p>In an interview with one of the editors in 1956, Hurley recalled the time and place of the meeting as indicated
                    above. He stated that Madame Chiang was
                    present at the meeting. According to Hurley’s recollection, Chiang asked whether Roosevelt and Churchill
                    were to meet with Stalin, to which
                        Hurley replied that such a meeting
                    was scheduled but not for Cairo. Hurley
                    also recalled discussing with Chiang the
                    pending plan for American-British-Chinese military cooperation in Burma.
                    (110.4–HD/12–1756.)</p>
            </div>
